`jsx/spread/child.js` is in the "Not idempotent" list of both Prettier conformance reports. Its own-line comment before `}` survives the first pass, but on the second pass it moves out of the spread child:

```jsx
// pass 1 (same as Prettier)
<div>
  {
    ...a
    //comment
  }
</div>;

// pass 2
<div>
  {
    ...a
  }
//comment
</div>;
```

`JSXExpressionContainer` prints the comments before `}` inside its braces (`FormatTrailingComments::Comments(comments_before(self.span.end))`), so `{a //comment}` is stable; `JSXSpreadChild` printed only the leading comments and the expression, so once the child sits on its own lines the comment is left for the parent to print after `}`. This adds the same trailing-comment print to `JSXSpreadChild`. (`JSXSpreadAttribute` already keeps it.)

Tests:
- New fixture `js/jsx/spread-child-trailing-comment.jsx`; it fails on `main`, and its output is identical to Prettier 3.9.10 (the pinned version) at both option sets and stable on a second pass.
- `jsx/spread/child.js` drops out of the "Not idempotent" lists in `conformance__prettier-js.snap` and `conformance__prettier-ts.snap`; no other conformance change.
- `cargo test -p oxc_formatter --all-features`, `cargo fmt --check`, `just lint -p oxc_formatter` (clippy with warnings denied) and `typos` pass.

AI disclosure: this PR (code, tests and description) was written with Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5); the verification above is what was run.

Closes #26928.

## Problem

`Symbol` and `Symbol.iterator` both configured as injects, module uses both forms. Only the member form gets replaced:

```js
// in
const supported = typeof Symbol !== "undefined" && typeof Symbol.iterator === "symbol";
const sym = Symbol("x");

// out, on main
import { symbolIterator as $inject_Symbol_iterator } from "./symbol-polyfill.js";
const supported = typeof Symbol !== "undefined" && typeof $inject_Symbol_iterator === "symbol";
const sym = Symbol("x");
```

`typeof Symbol` and `Symbol("x")` still reach the global. esbuild and `@rollup/plugin-inject` replace all three.

`inject_global_variables.rs:194` dropped a bare inject once any member inject rooted at the same name had been replaced. That handles `Buffer` + `Buffer.isBuffer`, where `Buffer.isBuffer('foo')` shouldn't pull in an unused `Buffer` import. It didn't check whether other references survived.

## Solution

`replace_dot_defines` records the `ReferenceId` of the object identifier each replaced member expression was rooted at. The bare inject stays when an unresolved reference to that name isn't one of them. `Buffer.isBuffer('foo')` alone still drops the import.

The issue suggests two approaches. Reference ids rather than counts, because `replaced_dot_defines` only records the first replacement per dot define, so a count is wrong for a module using `Symbol.iterator` twice.

