A generic function type right after the `<` of a type assertion is printed without parens, so the output starts with `<<` and no longer parses:

```ts
// input (parses)
var a = < <T>(x: T) => T>f;
var g = < <T>() => T>< <U>() => U>h;

// main (tsc: "Expression expected")
var a = <<T>(x: T) => T>f;
var g = <<T>() => T>(<<U>() => U>h);
```

In an expression, `<<` is read as a left shift, so the type assertion is gone. Prettier 3.9.10 prints the same, so this follows Prettier into invalid code. `TSFunctionType::needs_parentheses` now returns true for a function type with type parameters whose parent is a `TSTypeAssertion`:

```ts
var a = <(<T>(x: T) => T)>f;
var g = <(<T>() => T)>(<(<U>() => U)>h);
```

Only that position needs it. In type arguments TypeScript re-scans `<<` as two tokens, so `Array<<T>() => T>`, `f<<T>() => T>()`, `new C<<T>() => T>()` and an instantiation expression all parse and keep their output. A function type without type parameters, a constructor type (`<new <T>() => T>f`) and a function type nested inside another type don't start with `<` and don't change.

This is a divergence from the pin, so it has a `DIVERGENCES.md#type-assertion-generic-function-parens` entry, under `semantics (Prettier's output no longer parses)`, and a pin fixture.

